// IAMBLICHUS, ON THE MYSTERIES, IN PLAIN WORDS: a close paraphrase of
// Porphyry's Epistle to Anebo and of each of the 140 chapters of the reply in
// Thomas Taylor's English (Chiswick, 1821), in his ten Sections. Two voices are
// kept apart, as the book keeps them: Porphyry's letter, which asks the
// questions ("Porphyry asks ..."), and the reply, written in the person of the
// Egyptian priest Abammon ("Abammon answers ..."). That the reply is by
// Iamblichus is the ancient ascription reported in the note Taylor prints on
// p. 17, not something the book says; here the speaker is called by the name
// the book gives him. Where Abammon repeats one of Porphyry's questions, the
// paraphrase says so. Words in square brackets in Taylor's English are his
// own additions to the Greek, and are followed where they help the sense.
// Taylor's footnotes are not paraphrased. Nothing here is offered as true of
// the world: it is what the English says.

export const PLAIN_IAMBLICHUS_MYSTERIES: Record<string, string> = {
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- The Epistle
  "greek.iamb.epistle":
    "Porphyry asks, in a letter to the Egyptian prophet Anebo, a long series of questions. Granting that there are gods, what marks off gods, daemons, heroes and souls: their activities, their passions, or the bodies and places assigned to them? If the gods cannot be acted on, why are rites performed as if they could be moved, appeased or compelled? Why are sun, moon and stars gods if gods have no body; why are some gods kind and others harmful; and by what sign is a god, angel, daemon or soul known to be present? What is divination, in dreams, in frenzy brought on by music, water or vapour, in visions, or through entrails, birds and stars: a god at work, or the soul's own passion? Why are the invoked powers commanded like inferiors and asked to help with unjust deeds; why do priests avoid animal food and corpses; and why are threats made to 'burst the heavens' or reveal the secrets of Isis? What do the Egyptian symbols mean (a god rising from mud, seated on the lotus, sailing in a ship), why are meaningless and foreign names preferred, and what do the Egyptians hold the first cause to be, if all is bound to fate? How is a person's own daemon given and found? Lastly, is there another way to happiness, or do those who consult the gods about slaves, land, marriage or trade deal only with a deceiving daemon?",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- Section I
  "greek.iamb.1.1":
    "Abammon answers that Hermes, the god of speech, was rightly held to be shared by all priests, which is why the ancestors signed their writings with his name. Porphyry did well to put his doubts about the gods to the priests. Abammon takes the letter written to his disciple Anebo as written to himself, and will reply: it would be wrong if Pythagoras, Plato, Democritus and Eudoxus learned from the sacred scribes of their day but Porphyry, who thinks as they did, were turned away now. Porphyry may take the answer as coming from Anebo, or from Abammon, or from any Egyptian prophet; better still, he should ignore who is speaking and judge only whether what is said is true. Abammon will first sort the questions by kind and say which traditions they come from: some from the wise men of the Chaldeans, some from the prophets of the Egyptians, some from the philosophers, some from opinions not worth naming, and some from common human notions. Because they are so mixed, they need careful handling.",
  "greek.iamb.1.2":
    "Abammon answers that he will set out the teachings of the Assyrians and his own views, drawing on the countless writings of the ancients and on what they gathered into one treatise about all knowledge of the divine. Philosophical questions he will answer from the ancient pillars of Hermes, which Plato and Pythagoras knew and built their philosophy on. Foreign or quarrelsome questions he will handle gently or show to be absurd; questions from common notions he will treat plainly. Matters that need experience of divine rites he will explain as far as words allow; matters of pure thought he will treat so as to purify the soul. He will answer each kind in its own way: theological questions theologically, theurgic ones theurgically, philosophical ones philosophically, questions about first causes from first principles, and questions of conduct ethically.",
  "greek.iamb.1.3":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's opening words, 'it must be granted that there are gods', by saying that this is the wrong way to put it. Knowledge of the gods is born with us and is part of what we are; it comes before any judgement, choice, reasoning or proof, and is bound up with the soul's natural longing for the good. Strictly, our contact with the divine is not knowledge at all, since knowing keeps the knower apart from the known; before that there is a single, natural union with the gods that cannot be separated from them. So it cannot be 'granted' as if it might be refused or doubted, and we are not in a position to accept or reject it: we are contained and filled by it, and we are what we are by knowing the gods. The same holds for the higher beings that follow the gods, daemons, heroes and pure souls: they are to be understood as fixed and steady, not as things we weigh up for and against. Porphyry treats knowledge of the divine like any other knowledge, to be argued from opposites as in debate, but it is different in kind: it was never granted or acquired, but has always been with the soul.",
  "greek.iamb.1.4":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question about the 'peculiarities' that set the higher kinds of being apart. If he means differences within one shared kind, as 'rational' and 'irrational' divide 'animal', there are none, because gods, daemons, heroes and souls share no common essence. If he means that each kind is simply different in its whole being, as earlier and later natures are, the question is sound. But it is badly ordered: he should have asked first how they differ in essence, then in power, and only then in activity, whereas he asks only about activities, the last and least of their differences. Nor do 'active and passive motions' apply, since none of these beings acts and suffers by turns; even the soul's motion is simple, not a mover moving itself. 'Accidents' do not apply either, since in these beings nothing is added on to their essence. And Porphyry has it backwards: essences produce activities, not activities essences. If each kind were one, theology would be thrown into confusion; since they differ in kind, they have no common boundary to compare. The way to define them is to look at what is analogous in each order, among the many kinds of gods, among daemons and heroes, and among souls.",
  "greek.iamb.1.5":
    "Abammon answers by setting out the two ends of the scale. There is the good itself, beyond essence, and the good that exists as the most ancient and honourable bodiless essence; this is the mark of the gods, shared by all who belong to them. Souls that rule and care for bodies do not have that essential good, but a share and habit of it, though a lasting one, unlike the changeable virtue of human beings. Between the two ends are two middle orders. Heroes stand above souls in power, virtue, beauty and greatness, yet are close to them. Daemons hang from the gods and are far below them; they serve the gods' will, bring the gods' hidden goodness into action, make what is beyond speech speakable and what is formless visible in forms, and pass on what they receive to the orders beneath them. These middle orders bind gods and souls together, keep everything connected from top to bottom, carry what descends and what rises, and fit each thing to all the rest. The distinction holds in essence, power and activity alike. Seen another way, to the gods belong unity, firmness, unmoved causation and separateness from all things; to souls belong division into many, giving and receiving, sharing in life and mixing with everything that comes to be.",
  "greek.iamb.1.6":
    "Abammon answers that the middle orders should now be plain, since they complete the unbroken link between the two ends, but he adds more. The race of daemons is many, yet mixed together without being confused, and holds the lower things under the form of the higher. The race of heroes presides over more obvious division, multitude and motion, while holding within it the higher gifts of unity, purity and steadiness. One middle order is next to the highest end, the other next to the lowest; it is natural that the one that starts from the best should reach down, and the one that touches the last should in some way share in what is above it. Having divided the four kinds in these two ways, he will from now on describe only the two ends, briefly, since the middle orders can be understood from them.",
  "greek.iamb.1.7":
    "Abammon answers by describing the two ends. The highest is supreme and perfect, does everything at once and in an instant, makes and governs all things without leaning towards them, and comes before all things as their cause; it holds the ends of every activity and essence, and is beyond every measure and form. The lowest, the soul, cannot do everything, nor all at once; it leans towards what it makes and governs, depends on the will of the gods, moves from one thing to another and from imperfect to perfect, is drawn by desire towards lesser things and is shaped by measures taken from them. Intellect, the king of all beings and the art that makes the universe, is always with the gods, whole and self-contained; the soul shares in a divided intellect, looks after the whole, cares for lifeless things and is born at different times into different forms. Order and beauty, or their cause, belong to the gods by nature, while the soul always has a share of them; the gods rule all beings, the soul only within set bounds. From these ends the qualities of daemons and heroes, which lie between and resemble both, can be understood.",
  "greek.iamb.1.8":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's suggestion that the kinds differ by the bodies they are assigned to, gods to the ether, daemons to the air and souls to the earth. That would be like defining Socrates by his tribe when he sits in the council: it is unworthy of beings that are by nature free. Bodies serve these causes; the higher beings are not in bodies but rule them from outside, give them every good they can take, and receive nothing from them, so they cannot be marked out by them. Porphyry's view would make bodies better than gods. It is true of individual souls that the body they hang from matches the life they chose before entering it, but with the higher beings it is the other way round: lower things exist in higher ones, bodies in the bodiless, and are governed by them; the heavenly revolutions are contained in the soul of the heavens, and the souls of the spheres in intellect. A bodiless essence cannot be divided by bodily qualities or places. Nothing stops the gods from being everywhere; true being is everywhere it wills. Porphyry's view would also destroy sacred rites and the communion of gods with men, by leaving the earth empty of the gods and making priests no wiser than anyone else. In truth the gods are held by nothing and hold all things; earthly things exist within the fullness of the gods and, when fit for it, possess the gods already present in them. He will nonetheless treat the question in a reasoned, theological way.",
  "greek.iamb.1.9":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's questions why theurgists call on gods of the earth and the underworld if the gods dwell only in the heavens, and how gods of infinite, undivided power can be assigned to the air, to places and to parts of bodies. It is not true that the gods are only in the heavens: all things are full of them. The answer to all such questions lies in how the gods hold their portions. Whether a god's lot is heaven or earth, a sacred city or region, a grove or a sacred statue, the god lights it from outside, as the sun does with its rays. Sunlight is in the air without mixing with it, since none stays when the sun has gone; it is one and whole everywhere and cannot be cut off or shut in. In the same way the world, which is divided, is divided around the one undivided light of the gods, which is present whole to everything able to receive it, fills all things and binds ends to beginnings. The heaven imitates this by turning in a circle and joining earth to heaven. Whoever looks at the world, that visible statue of the gods, should be ashamed to divide the gods into parts and boundaries. What does differ is the things that share in them: some receive the gods in the manner of the ether, some of the air, some of water, and the sacred art uses invocations suited to each.",
  "greek.iamb.1.10":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's division of the higher beings into the passive and the impassive by rejecting it. None of them is passive, and none is impassive in the sense of being able to suffer but freed from it by virtue; they are wholly outside the opposition of acting and suffering, and are by nature unchanging. Take the lowest of them, a soul wholly freed from bodies: it has no need of pleasure, which restores the body, nor of pain, which breaks it down, nor of the bodily stirrings that come before sensation, since it is not held in a body. Even when the rational soul comes to a body, neither it nor the forms it gives the body suffer; it is the thing that shares in the soul, the composite, that suffers. As the soul is the source of living things that are born and die, yet is itself unborn and undying, so it remains unchanged while what shares in it suffers. If even the soul cannot suffer, still less can daemons and heroes, who are everlasting attendants of the gods and keep their order. Passion is disorderly and unstable, and belongs to some other kind of being than those that always exist and depend on the gods. So daemons, and all the higher kinds that follow them, are impassive.",
  "greek.iamb.1.11":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question why so much is done in the rites as if the gods could be acted on. This comes from not knowing the priests' mysteries. Of the things always done in the rites, some have a hidden cause higher than reason; some have been consecrated as symbols of the higher beings from eternity; some keep another kind of image, as nature expresses unseen forms in visible shapes; some are done for honour, or for likeness and kinship; and some help us, purify our passions or turn away harm. None of it means that gods or daemons are passive, for a bodiless, everlasting being cannot be changed by bodies, nor does it need human worship, being full in itself. As for the particular rites: the raising of phalli is a sign of the power of generation, called out to make the world fruitful, which is why many are set up in spring. The obscene words spoken show the ugliness of matter before it is given form, so that those who use them turn their desire towards its opposite. There is another reason too: passions held back wholly grow fiercer, but let out a little at a time they are satisfied and calmed. So in comedy and tragedy we watch others' passions and are purged of our own, and in the rites certain things seen and heard free us from the harm they would do in act. Heraclitus rightly called these things remedies.",
  "greek.iamb.1.12":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's claim that invocations are made to the gods as if they could be acted on, so that gods as well as daemons would be passive. The light that comes through invocations appears of itself and is complete in itself; it has nothing to do with being drawn down, and comes through the gods' own activity, as far above our will as the divine will of the good is above a chosen life. By that will the gods, kind and gracious, give their light to theurgists without stint, call their souls up to themselves, unite them with themselves, and train them, while still in the body, to be separated from it and turned towards their eternal source. The effects show that this is the soul's salvation: in seeing these blessed sights the soul takes on another life and is no longer counted as merely human. Invocation does not drag pure gods down to what is impure; it makes us, who have become passive through birth, pure and unchanging. Even prayers asking the gods to 'incline' to us do not bend the gods' mind towards men, but fit the human will to share in the gods and raise it to them. For this reason the names of the gods used in the rites, and other divine symbols, have the power to lift up and to join invocations to the gods themselves.",
  "greek.iamb.1.13":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's point about 'appeasing the anger' of the gods by saying what that anger is. It is not an old, settled rage, as some think, but our own turning away from the gods' kindly care, as if we hid from midday light in the dark and robbed ourselves of their gifts. Appeasing them turns us back to share in the divine and in the gods' care, and binds those who share to what they share in; so far from working through passion, it frees us from the troubled state of being cut off from the gods. Likewise the 'offering of victims', when some evil is present on earth, brings a remedy and protects us from harm and change: whether through gods or daemons, it calls on them as the drivers-out of evil and our true saviours. The powers that avert punishments of nature and of birth do not do so through passions. And if the withdrawal of the gods' guardianship seems to bring harm of itself, the persuasion of appeasement recalls the goodwill of the higher kinds to care for us again, and takes away our lack of good, while itself remaining pure and unchanged.",
  "greek.iamb.1.14":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's point about the so-called 'necessities of the gods'. These necessities belong to the gods and fit their nature; they do not come from outside or by force. Just as the good must be good by necessity, so the gods are wholly what they are and cannot be otherwise. This necessity is joined with kindly will and is a friend of love; it keeps the gods' order unchanged and stays within its one bound. So the opposite of what Porphyry concludes is true: if theurgy really has the powers Abammon has shown it has, the divine is not lured, not moved and not compelled.",
  "greek.iamb.1.15":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's division of the gods as 'pure intellects' from daemons as 'souls sharing in intellect'. It confuses daemons with souls, which also share in intellect, and the gods with an immaterial intellect, which they surpass. To the doubt whether one should pray to pure intellects, which cannot be swayed and have no senses, he replies that one should pray to no others: what is divine and one in us is roused in prayer, seeks what is like it and is joined to perfection. The gods do not take in prayers through organs; the first causes hold all things at once, and hold within themselves the activity of pious invocations, especially those joined to them by the rites. Supplication is not foreign to them: the sense of our own nothingness drives us to pray, and by praying we grow like what we pray to and slowly exchange our weakness for its perfection. Sacred prayers were sent to men by the gods as their symbols. And since the gods share in bodiless forms and measures, any kinship, near or far, is enough for contact, for they are present at once to whatever is fitted to them.",
  "greek.iamb.1.16":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's division of gods from daemons as bodiless and bodily. It is a commoner division than the last, but it tells nothing of their essence, nor even gives a guess at it: it does not say whether they are living beings, or lifeless, or above needing life. Nor is it clear how the words are meant. If 'bodiless' is one shared class, it is absurd to put a line and time, gods, fire and water in it together; if it covers many different things, why should 'bodiless' mean gods rather than points, or 'bodily' mean daemons rather than earth? And it is not said whether gods and daemons have bodies, ride in them as in a vehicle, use them, contain them or are the same as them. But perhaps it should not be examined too closely, since Porphyry gives it as other people's view, not his own.",
  "greek.iamb.1.17":
    "Abammon answers the objection that might follow: how can the sun, the moon and the visible beings in the heavens be gods, if gods are bodiless? The heavenly gods are not held by bodies; they hold bodies within their divine life and activity. They do not turn towards the body; the body turns towards its divine cause, and it does not hinder their bodiless perfection or trouble them. It needs no attention, but follows them of itself, rising together with them towards the one of the gods. A heavenly body is also the kind of body most like the gods: as they are one, it is simple; as they are undivided, it cannot be split; as they are unchanging, it does not alter; as their activity is uniform, it has one circuit; its unvarying motion copies their sameness, and its own life copies theirs. It is not made of opposites as our bodies are, nor are soul and body joined in it as two things; the heavenly living beings are wholes of one kind, in which the higher always rules and the divine form prevails throughout. So the visible heavenly beings are all gods, and in a certain way bodiless.",
  "greek.iamb.1.18":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question how some gods can be kindly and others harmful, a view taken from the casters of birth charts. All the gods are good and always cause good. The powers of the heavenly bodies come down unchanged and impassive, but the world of coming-to-be, varied and made of opposites, receives what is one in a divided, hostile way and what is impassive in a passive way, according to its own nature. So it is as absurd to call the heavenly bodies evil because their effects are sometimes evil as to give colour and shape to intelligible forms because what shares in them is coloured and shaped. Saturn's outflow binds and Mars's moves, but matter receives the one as freezing and the other as burning: like a sick man who cannot bear the sun's heat and calls the sun bad for health. What preserves the whole may harm a part, as dancers sometimes knock against one another in a dance. Decay and change belong to parts, not to wholes and first causes, so neither the heavenly gods nor their gifts cause evil.",
  "greek.iamb.1.19":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question what joins the gods with bodies in the heavens to the bodiless gods. If the heavenly gods, being bodiless, ride in the spheres, their source is in the intelligible world, and they rule the heavens by one unbounded activity, present to them while staying separate and unmixed. More exactly, the visible statues of the gods, the heavenly bodies, come from divine intelligible patterns, rest in them and take their image from them; they in turn shape the order below the moon; the divine forms of intellect present to them exist before them apart; and their intelligible patterns above the heavens remain in unity, all things at once. So there is one undivided bond between them, both of intellectual activity and of shared forms, with nothing in between to cut them off, since what is bodiless is not divided by place. The going out from the one and the return of all things to it, and the turning of later gods back to earlier ones, which give them the same essence and power, hold the whole company in unbreakable union. The higher one rises from parts to wholes, the more one finds an eternal union. In the gods, beginning, middle and end exist together in the one, so there is no asking from where the one comes to them: their very being is that one. So the bodiless gods are united with the visible gods, who are themselves outside their bodies and so in the intelligible world; it would be strange if it were otherwise.",
  "greek.iamb.1.20":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question what distinguishes daemons from the visible and invisible gods, though he has answered it in part already, since it is good to speak often of fine things. The visible gods are united with the intelligible gods and share their form, but daemons are far from them in essence and only faintly resemble them; that sets daemons apart from the visible gods. From the invisible gods they differ in the very way they are invisible: daemons are invisible because the senses cannot grasp them, but the gods because they are beyond reasoning and material understanding. The invisible gods are not, as invisible, any greater than the visible ones, for what is divine has the same power wherever it is, and no place changes the gods' rule. A further difference: all the gods, visible and invisible, hold the government of everything in heaven and the world, while the powers with a daemonic charge govern particular portions of the world, have a divided essence and power, and are bound up with what they govern. The gods, even when they ride in bodies, are wholly separate from them, and caring for bodies takes nothing from them; but clinging to the realm of birth and being divided about it puts daemons lower. The divine rules; the daemonic serves, carrying out with ready hands what the gods understand, will and command.",
  "greek.iamb.1.21":
    "Abammon answers by returning to Porphyry's division of passive from impassive, which is wrong for the reasons given and also because it argues from the rites that the higher kinds can be acted on. No rite performed by the priestly laws works through passion or satisfies passions. Each was laid down from the beginning by the gods' sacred laws and in an intellectual way; each copies the intelligible and heavenly order of the gods and holds the eternal measures of beings and the wonderful signs sent here by the maker and father of all, by which what cannot be spoken is brought to light in hidden symbols, what is formless is mastered by forms, what is above every image is expressed in images, and all is done by a divine cause so far above passions that reason cannot touch it. This is why people go astray: unable to grasp the reasons of the rites but thinking they can, they judge divine things by their own human passions, and so fall away from the divine and drag it down to human feeling. Kneeling, worship, gifts and first-fruits offered to gods and to men should not be thought of in the same way: those offered to men are human and stir passions on both sides, but those offered to the gods should be honoured as done in unchanging awe and reverence.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- Section II
  "greek.iamb.2.1":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question how daemons, heroes and souls differ, whether in essence, power or activity. Daemons are produced by the generative and creative powers of the gods, at the farthest end of their going out and division into parts. Heroes are produced by the principles of life in the divine, and from them the first and perfect measures of souls take their bounds. Since they come from different causes, their essences differ: the essence of daemons makes and perfects the things of the world and completes the oversight of each thing that comes to be; the essence of heroes is living and rational, and leads souls. Their powers differ too: daemons' powers are generative, watch over nature and over the bond that ties souls to bodies; heroes' powers give life, lead men and are free of the realm of birth.",
  "greek.iamb.2.2":
    "Abammon answers by defining their activities. Daemons' activities concern the world and reach further; heroes' reach less far and are turned towards the order of souls. After these comes the soul, at the end of the divine orders. It takes some powers from both, adds further gifts of its own, puts forth different forms, principles and ways of life at different times from different sources, takes on various lives in each part of the world, joins and leaves what it likes, becomes like all things while staying distinct from them, and is joined to the gods by other harmonies than those that join daemons and heroes to them. It has a less lasting life and activity than they, yet by the gods' goodwill and light it often rises higher, to the order of angels, and is then wholly perfected into an angelic soul and a pure life. So the soul seems to hold every kind of essence and form; but in truth it is always defined by one thing, and is joined to different higher causes at different times. With differences so great, it is no longer a question what separates them: each is known by its own nature, and what they share is seen where they come together.",
  "greek.iamb.2.3":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question by what sign the presence of a god, angel, archangel, daemon, archon or soul can be known: each appears as it is, in keeping with its essence, power and activity. The gods' apparitions are of one form, daemons' varied, angels' simpler than daemons' but below the gods', archangels' nearer the divine causes; archons', if they rule the elements below the moon, varied but orderly, if they preside over matter, more varied and imperfect; souls' of every kind. The gods' apparitions shine with saving light; archangels' are terrible yet gentle; angels' gentler; daemons' fearful; heroes' (which Porphyry left out) gentler than daemons'; material archons' harmful. The gods' never change in size or shape, and angels' are unchanging too; daemons' are seen now large, now small, and material archons' change form in many ways. Order and calm belong to the gods, disturbance to daemons. Divine beauty shines with immense splendour and gives divine joy; archangels' beauty comes next, angels' is divided, daemons' and heroes' lies in definite forms, some archons' is counterfeit, and souls' is more divided still. Each shares in beauty according to its rank.",
  "greek.iamb.2.4":
    "Abammon answers by continuing the marks of each kind. The gods' activities are swifter than intellect yet unmoved; archangels' mix swiftness with action; angels' have some motion; daemons' seem swifter than they are; heroes' are grand but slower; souls' more mobile but weaker. When the gods appear their size can hide the sky, the sun and the moon, and the earth shakes as they come down; before archangels a divided light runs; angels' light is less, daemons' more divided and varying in size; world-ruling archons appear immeasurably great, material archons proud and arrogant, souls smaller than heroes. The gods' self-appearing images are the clearest sights of truth; archangels' are true and perfect, angels' less full, daemons' obscure, souls' shadowy. The gods' fire shines with an undivided, ineffable light that fills the depths of the world, and is wholly steady; archangels' fire is calm and has a great multitude about it; angels' is divided and steadily moving; daemons' is a turbid fire, smaller and unstable; heroes' is quick-moving; souls' is divided, many-formed and in constant change.",
  "greek.iamb.2.5":
    "Abammon answers by giving further marks. The power that purifies souls is perfect in the gods; in archangels it raises them up; angels alone loosen the bonds of birth; daemons draw souls down into nature; heroes lead them to care for the works of the senses; archons hand them the government of worldly affairs or the oversight of material things; and souls, when they appear, lean towards birth. Whatever is pure and stable in the visible image belongs to the higher kinds: what is supremely bright and rests in itself, to the gods; what is bright but rests in another, to archangels; what stays in another, to angels; what is borne along at random, unsettled and full of foreign stuff, to the lower orders. They differ also by what is mixed with them: worldly vapours with daemons, moving against the motion of the world; compounds of airy substances with heroes; the world-ruling archons stay the same; the archons of matter are full of matter; and souls are full of stains and foreign spirits. Lastly, with the gods matter is burned up at once; with archangels in a short time; angels release from it and lift above it; daemons adorn it; heroes fit themselves to it with care; world-ruling archons are present to it from above, material archons are full of it; pure souls appear outside matter, impure ones surrounded by it.",
  "greek.iamb.2.6":
    "Abammon answers by describing what each appearance gives. The presence of the gods gives health of body, virtue of soul and purity of mind, and raises everything in us to its source; it destroys what is cold and destructive in us, strengthens what is hot, brings all into harmony with soul and intellect, and shows what is not body as body to the soul's eyes through the body's. Archangels give the same, but not always, not in everything, and not completely or securely, and with a lesser light. Angels give still more divided goods, with a light far from perfect. Daemons make the body heavy, bring diseases, draw the soul down to nature, cling to bodies and the senses, hold back on earth those hurrying towards the divine fire, and do not free them from Fate. Heroes are like daemons in other ways, but stir people to noble and great deeds. World-ruling archons give the goods of the world and of human life; material archons give material benefits and earthly works. The sight of pure souls established among the angels raises up and saves the soul, brings sacred hope and its goods; the sight of other souls draws down to birth, spoils that hope, and fills viewers with passions that tie them to the body.",
  "greek.iamb.2.7":
    "Abammon answers by describing the company each power appears with, which shows its rank. Gods are surrounded by gods or angels; archangels have angels going before, beside or behind them, or a guard of other angels; angels show the works of their own order; good daemons show their works and benefits, avenging daemons the kinds of punishment they inflict, and wicked daemons are surrounded by harmful, blood-drinking, savage beasts. Archons of the first rank show parts of the world, other archons draw to themselves the disorder of matter. A soul that is a whole, of no particular kind, shows a formless fire spread through the whole world, a sign of the one undivided soul of the universe; a purified soul shows a fiery form, a pure, unmixed fire, its inmost light and a steady shape, and gladly follows its guide upward; but a soul that sinks downward drags signs of bonds and punishments, is heavy with material spirits, and shows the daemons who preside over birth before it. The airy kinds show airy fire, the earthly a darker fire, the heavenly a brighter one; and all kinds are ordered in three ranks, first, middle and last, with the gods as the highest causes, angels depending on archangels, daemons serving angels and heroes serving in turn, and souls appearing as the last of the higher kinds, together with places that match their rank.",
  "greek.iamb.2.8":
    "Abammon answers by describing how fine and thin the light of each is. The gods send out a light so fine that bodily eyes cannot bear it: those who see the divine fire cannot breathe and grow faint, like fish drawn up from thick water into thin air. Archangels' light is also hard to breathe in, but less pure and overwhelming. The presence of angels makes the air bearable, so that theurgists can be united with it. When daemons are present the air is not changed at all, made no thinner, with no light going before them and no splendour spreading round them. When heroes appear, parts of the earth are moved and sounds are heard around them, but the air is not made too thin for theurgists. Around archons runs a crowd of luminous appearances hard to bear, but without the fineness of what is above the world. And the air is most akin to the appearances of souls, and takes their outline.",
  "greek.iamb.2.9":
    "Abammon answers by describing what each appearance does to the soul of those who call on it. When the gods appear, the soul is freed from passions, gains a surpassing perfection and a higher activity, and shares in divine love and immense joy. With archangels it gains a pure state, intellectual contemplation and unchanging power; with angels, wisdom and truth, pure virtue, steady knowledge and due order. With daemons it receives the desire of birth and of nature, a wish to carry out the works of Fate and the power to do them; with heroes, impulses towards the fellowship of souls; with archons, motions stirred in the soul; and with the sight of souls, a leaning towards birth and care for bodies. The gods' appearance gives truth, power, right action and the greatest goods; archangels give truth about some things, sometimes, in some place, to some person, and a limited power; angels divide their gifts more narrowly still; daemons give not goods of the soul but goods of the body, when the order of the world allows; heroes give goods of the second and third rank, and look to the earthly community of souls; archons give worldly benefits or material advantages; and souls give things useful to human life. That, he says, answers Porphyry's questions about the appearances.",
  "greek.iamb.2.10":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's claim that boasting and showing a shadowy phantom are common to gods, daemons and all the higher kinds. It is not so. A god, an angel or a good daemon tells men what its own nature is and never claims more than its power or its good; truth goes with the gods as light with the sun, and the divine needs no praise added in words. Boasting happens when something goes wrong in the theurgic art: when the right self-showing images do not appear but others do, lower powers take on the forms of higher orders, pretend to be them and speak arrogantly beyond their rank. The priest must learn from the whole order of the apparitions how to expose and reject such pretenders. One should not judge an art by its failures; things hard to do rightly even with endless labour should not be described by the mistakes made in ignorance. The beings that give truth to all others show their own truth first, and show theurgists a fire visible in itself; heat does not chill, nor light darken. Phantoms are like reflections in water or a mirror, imitations that deceive and draw the mind away from true knowledge of the gods. The gods do not change themselves into phantoms or send them out, since that would serve no purpose and is against their nature; they send true images of themselves by illumination, and their attendants follow them in this. Porphyry's view would mix up all the higher kinds and leave no difference between gods and daemons.",
  "greek.iamb.2.11":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's statement that ignorance and error about these things are impiety and impurity, which everyone accepts, by adding what it leaves out, from the side of the priests' art rather than of philosophy. Even if ignorance is impious, it does not follow that offerings and divine works are false. It is not thinking that joins theurgists to the gods, or else philosophers who only theorise would have theurgic union with them. What gives that union is the perfect working of unspeakable rites, done in a divine way beyond all understanding, and the power of unexplainable symbols known only to the gods. When we are not thinking, the tokens (synthemata) do their own work by themselves, and the gods' power knows its own images by itself, not because our thought stirs it, for the whole is not moved by its parts. Our thoughts, our best states of soul and our purity are only helping causes; what truly rouses the divine will is the divine tokens themselves. So the power of the rites is not in our hands, their truth does not lie in our ideas, nor their falsehood in our mistakes. Union is not achieved without knowledge, but knowledge is not the same as union, and divine purity is not gained by right knowledge, any more than bodily purity by health. He then passes over Porphyry's remarks that knowledge of the gods is light and ignorance darkness, and turns to divination.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- Section III
  "greek.iamb.3.1":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's request to explain what happens in foreknowing the future by saying that, as put, the question cannot be answered: it treats foreknowledge as something that comes into being in nature. It is not; it is not a natural change, nor an invention for the needs of life, nor a human work at all, but divine and above nature, sent from the heavens, unborn and eternal. The cure for all such doubts is to know where divination comes from: not from bodies or their passions, not from nature or its powers, not from human skill or an art learned from outside. All its authority belongs to the gods, who give it through divine works or signs, divine sights and knowledge; everything in our soul and body and in nature serves only as an instrument, and places and the like as its matter. Whoever leaves out the first causes and puts divination down to bodily motions, changes of passion, human activity or natural principles goes wholly astray. Foreknowledge cannot come without cause from things that have none; it must be seen as coming from the gods, who hold in themselves the bounds of all knowledge, and spread through the whole world. From that one source all the kinds of divination can be understood, as he will now show in answer to Porphyry's questions.",
  "greek.iamb.3.2":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's account of dreams, that in sleep we often learn the future, less clearly than when awake. That is true of human dreams, which come from our thoughts, imagination or the day's cares, and are sometimes true and sometimes false. Dreams sent by a god are different. They come as sleep is leaving us: a voice tells us briefly what to do, heard between sleeping and waking or when fully awake. Sometimes an invisible, bodiless spirit surrounds those lying down, perceived by another kind of awareness, coming with a rushing sound and freeing soul and body from their passions. Sometimes a bright, calm light holds the eyes closed while the other senses stay awake, so that the sleepers hear what the gods say. Wakefulness, the holding of the eyes, heaviness of the head and sudden rousing are all signs sent by the gods. So Porphyry should drop 'being asleep' and 'less clearly' from divine dreams: in them the gods are present at least as clearly as in waking. Those who confuse them with human dreams foretell the future only rarely and by chance, and so doubt that dreams hold any truth.",
  "greek.iamb.3.3":
    "Abammon answers by giving the teaching of the wise about dreams. The soul has two lives, one with the body and one separate from all body. Awake we mostly use the first; asleep we are freed as if from bonds and use the life apart from birth, intellectual or divine. Since intellect sees true beings and the soul holds the principles of all that comes to be, the soul naturally foreknows what is laid down in their causes. Its divination is most true when it is united with the gods: then it receives from them knowledge of what has been and will be, sees the whole of time, and is given the care of setting right what happens in it. So it heals the sick and often hands down arts, rules of justice and laws. In the temple of Asclepius diseases are healed through divine dreams; Alexander's army was saved when Bacchus appeared in a dream; and the city of Aphytis, besieged by Lysander, was saved by a dream sent to him by Jupiter Ammon, after which he lifted the siege. Such things happen daily, and show what divine divination in sleep is and how it helps mankind.",
  "greek.iamb.3.4":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's statement that many foretell the future in inspired frenzy while awake and using their senses, yet are not aware of their state, or less than before. He gives the signs of those truly possessed by the gods. They either give over their whole life to the inspiring gods as a vehicle or instrument, or exchange the human life for the divine, or act with their own life towards the god; but they do not act through the senses, nor are they awake like people roused from sleep, nor moved by impulse, nor aware of their state, nor using any knowledge of their own. The greatest proof is this: many in divine inspiration are not burned when fire is put to them, because the inspiring power keeps it off; many who are burned do not notice, because they are not then living the life of an animal; some are pierced with spits, struck on the shoulders with axes or cut on the arms with knives without knowing it. What they do is not human: they reach places no one can reach, are thrown into fire and walk through it, and cross rivers unharmed, like the priestess at Castabala. This shows that the inspired are not conscious of their state, and live not a human or animal life of sense and impulse, but a more divine life by which they are wholly possessed.",
  "greek.iamb.3.5":
    "Abammon answers that there are many kinds of divine possession, and so many different signs of it. The inspiration differs as the gods who inspire differ, or as the manner of possession differs: the god possesses us, or we give ourselves wholly to the god, or we act together with him. We may share in his last, middle or first power; there may be sharing only, or communion, or union. The soul alone may receive it, or the soul with the body, or the whole living being. So the signs vary: movements of the whole body or of parts, or complete stillness; ordered steps and dances, beautiful sounds, or their opposites; the body seen to rise, swell, or be carried up into the air, or the opposite; a voice steady in strength, or greatly varied after silences; sounds rising and falling musically, or strained and slackened in other ways.",
  "greek.iamb.3.6":
    "Abammon answers that the most important sign is this: the one who draws down a divinity sees a spirit coming down and entering someone, knows its size and kind, and is mysteriously guided by it. Before the spirit is received, the recipient sees a form of fire, sometimes visible to everyone present, as the god arrives or departs; from this sight those who know learn the god's truth, power and rank, on what matters he can speak truly, and what he can give and do. Those who draw down spirits invisibly, without these blessed sights, are as if in the dark and know nothing of what they do beyond a few signs in the body of the inspired person; the most important things are hidden from them. If the fire of the gods and an ineffable light come from outside to the possessed person, fill him, master him and surround him, so that he cannot act for himself, what sense, attention or thought of his own can he have? What human motion, passion, ecstasy or wandering of the imagination can there be? These are the divine signs of true inspiration.",
  "greek.iamb.3.7":
    "Abammon answers that one must also know what inspiration (enthusiasm) is and how it comes about. It is wrongly thought to be a movement of the mind together with daemonic inspiration: in it the human mind is not moved, and the inspiration comes from the gods, not daemons. Nor is it simply ecstasy, standing outside oneself: it is a lifting up and passing over to a better state, while madness and ecstasy are a turning to the worse. Those who speak of ecstasy describe what happens to the inspired but not what comes first, which is being wholly possessed by the god, the loss of one's own mind following after. Inspiration is not a matter of the soul or any of its powers, nor of intellect, nor of bodily weakness: it is not a human work, and human powers serve only as the subject and instrument the god uses. The god does the whole work of divination himself, unmixed with anything else, while soul and body are not moved at all. When that is so, divination is free of falsehood; when the soul has been disturbed or is moved meanwhile, or the body gets in the way and upsets the divine harmony, divination becomes confused and false, and the inspiration is no longer true.",
  "greek.iamb.3.8":
    "Abammon answers that if true divination were the divine part of the soul set loose from the rest, or intellect separated or stretched, or a heightening of activity or passion, or a sharpening of thought, or a heat of the mind, it could fairly be called the soul's offspring. If its cause were the body, through a melancholy or other temperament, through heat, cold or moisture, or their mixture, or the soul's airy part, it would be a bodily passion stirred by natural motions; and if both, a motion of the whole living being. But inspired activity is the work of neither body nor soul nor both together, which hold no cause of divine madness, for the better cannot be born of the worse. Its causes are the illuminations that come from the gods, the spirits they send, and the god's complete mastery, which takes hold of everything in us and wholly removes our own awareness and motion. This possession utters words that the speakers do not understand: they speak, as is said, 'with a raving mouth', wholly serving and yielding to the god who rules them. That is the nature of inspiration and its causes, though he says this is not stated with complete precision.",
  "greek.iamb.3.9":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's remark that some become inspired on hearing cymbals, drums or a certain melody, such as the Corybantic dancers and those possessed by Sabazius or the Mother of the Gods. Music does stir the feelings, can cause or cure disordered passions, and some melodies bring on Bacchic frenzy while others end it; but all this is natural and human and has nothing divine to do with inspiration. Rather, sounds and melodies are consecrated to the gods and akin to their orders and powers and to the harmonious motions of the universe. Through fitting melodies the gods themselves become present, since whatever has even a chance likeness to them shares in them at once, and complete possession follows. This is not because body and soul feel with the music, but because the gods' inspiration is bound up with divine harmony from the beginning, so it is no purging or medicine. Nor is the soul made of harmony and rhythm; rather, before it gave itself to the body it heard the divine harmony, and when in the body it hears melodies that keep traces of it, it welcomes them, remembers the divine harmony through them, and shares in it as far as it can.",
  "greek.iamb.3.10":
    "Abammon answers by going into this kind of divination more closely. It is not that nature draws each thing to its like, nor that the state of the air changes the bodies of the inspired, nor that inspiration fits passions and things that come to be: the gods' gift is impassive and above birth. The power of the Corybantes guards and acts, while that of Sabazius belongs to Bacchic inspiration, the purification of souls and release from ancient divine anger, so their inspirations are wholly different. Porphyry seems to think those possessed by the Mother of the Gods are men, but they are mostly women; this inspiration gives life and fills, and differs from all other madness. So too the inspirations of the Nymphs, of Pan and others can be told apart by the powers of the gods, and why the inspired leap, dwell on mountains or appear bound can be explained from divine causes. It is not that some excess of body or soul needs purging, nor that the seasons cause such states: such ideas are bodily and far from a divine life. The spirits roused by the gods drive out every human and natural motion, and their workings must be traced to first, divine causes.",
  "greek.iamb.3.11":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's mention of three famous oracles: Clarian Apollo at Colophon, where water is drunk; Delphi, where the prophetess sits at the mouth of a cave; and Branchidae, where she breathes the vapour of water. At Colophon the prophetess drinks, after many rites, from a spring in an underground room and gives oracles unseen. The water does not carry a prophetic spirit; the god surrounds the spring from outside, fills it with prophetic power, and uses the prophetess as an instrument, so that she does not know what she says. She first fasts a day and night and withdraws to holy places. At Delphi, whether she is filled by a fiery spirit from the cave or sits on a bronze tripod or a four-legged stool, she gives herself wholly to a divine spirit and is lit by a ray of divine fire; the god is distinct from the fire, the spirit, the seat and all the apparatus of the place. At Branchidae, whether she holds a wand, sits on an axle, wets her feet or her robe, or breathes the vapour, all these fit her to receive the god from outside. The sacrifices, baths and fasting show that the god is called by prayer and comes from outside: an older god, separate from the place and the cause of the whole divination.",
  "greek.iamb.3.12":
    "Abammon answers that the divination of oracles agrees with everything said so far about foretelling. If this power were bound to the nature of places and bodies, or moved by a motion measured by number, it could not foreknow, always and alike, things that exist everywhere and at all times. Being separate and free from places and from what is measured by time, it is present to all things wherever they are, exists together with everything that comes to be in time, and holds the truth of all things in one. So the gods' prophetic power is not held in parts by any place, any human body or any soul confined to one kind of divided nature; it is wholly present, undivided, to all that can receive it, lights and fills all things from outside, passes through all the elements, earth, air, fire and water, and gives some share of foreknowledge to every living thing and natural product, more to some and less to others. Existing before all things, it is able to fill all things, as far as each can take part in it.",
  "greek.iamb.3.13":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's mention of a private kind of divination, 'some become inspired by standing on characters, as those filled by the entry of spirits'. Because of those who misuse it, this kind is hard to define; it is shallow, widely known, full of intolerable falsehood and deceit, and no god is present in it. It stirs a motion of the soul hostile to the gods and draws from them a faint, shadowy image which, being weak, is easily disturbed by wicked daemonic spirits. A true image of the gods is pure, unchanging and true, and no opposing spirit can reach or hinder it: as darkness cannot bear the sunlight but vanishes at once, so when the gods' power shines out there is no room for the tumult of evil spirits, which fades into nothing. Porphyry's own words, 'standing on characters', point to the cause of all the trouble: some people ignore the whole science of the rites, both the invoking priest and the overseer (epoptes), despise the order of religion and the long, holy labours, reject the sacred laws and ceremonies, and think it enough to stand on characters for an hour to make a spirit enter. How could anything good or complete come of that, or how could fleeting acts touch the eternal being of the gods? Such rash people are wholly wrong and do not deserve to be counted as diviners.",
  "greek.iamb.3.14":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's account of those who stay aware but are inspired in their imagination, helped by darkness, potions, incantations or compositions, and see visions in water, on a wall, in the air, or in the light of the sun or another heavenly body. All of this can be called one thing, the 'drawing in of light'. Light shines on the soul's airy, luminous vehicle, and from it divine visions take hold of the imagination by the gods' will. Either the gods are present to the soul, or they send a light ahead of them; either way the divine stays separate. Attention and reasoning know what is happening, but the imagination is inspired, moved by the gods and not by itself. So those who bring in light use darkness as a helper, or the light of the sun, the moon or the air; sometimes arrangements suited to the gods who are to come, or incantations; sometimes water, which takes light well; sometimes a wall prepared with sacred characters to fix the light in one place. All come down to illumination from outside, obeying only the gods' will and shining from the ether, the air, the moon, the sun or another sphere, so this divination is free, primary and worthy of the gods.",
  "greek.iamb.3.15":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's mention of divination by human art, through entrails, birds and stars, which has much of guesswork and opinion in it. In general this art uses divine signs, brought about by the gods in various ways, and from the likeness between things and the signs shown it judges and predicts by probability. The gods produce the signs either through nature, which serves the coming-to-be of things in general and in particular, or through the daemons of birth who preside over the elements, single bodies and everything in the world and readily bring about what appears, as the gods will. The signs foreshow the gods' decrees and the future symbolically, as Heraclitus says, 'neither speaking nor concealing, but signifying', because they express the way things are made by showing it in advance. As the gods make all things through forms, so they signify all things through signs, impressed as by a seal (dia synthematon); perhaps they also sharpen our minds in this way.",
  "greek.iamb.3.16":
    "Abammon answers by going into particulars. The animal's soul, the daemon set over it, the air and the turning of the heavens change the entrails in accordance with the gods' will; a sign of this is that entrails are often found without a heart or other vital parts. Birds are moved by their own souls, and led by the daemon over animals, the motion of the air and the power of the heavens to what the gods ordained; the greatest sign is that birds sometimes hurl themselves to the ground and die, which is against nature. The movements of the stars come close to the eternal circuits of the heavens and are moved at the bidding of the heavenly gods, and outflows of the heavenly bodies pass into the air. The unity and sympathy of the universe, in which the farthest parts move together as in one living being, carry these signs from the gods to men, first through the heavens and then through the air. So the gods send signs through many instruments, daemons, souls and all of nature, while staying separate from the world of birth; the signs and all divination come from the gods' mind and return to it.",
  "greek.iamb.3.17":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's doubt whether the divine serves men so far as to let some become diviners 'from meal'. To call the gods' care for us 'service' misunderstands their power and goodness. Divine activity is not drawn down to us; it stays above, gives itself to what shares in it without being lessened, and uses all things as its servants. Porphyry pictures the gods' works as like ours, since we are drawn into what we care for; but the gods' power is never drawn down in making the worlds or in foretelling. The more it stays in itself, the fuller it is, holding all things while held by none, and it produces all kinds of divination at once in one will. That the gods' foreshowing reaches lifeless things, pebbles, rods, wood, stones, corn or wheat, is the most wonderful thing about it: it gives soul to the lifeless and makes what has no reason a bearer of meaning. As the god sometimes makes a foolish man speak wisely, to show that the work is divine, so through things without knowledge he reveals thoughts older than all knowledge. Divination is therefore ruling and primary, and the divine is not drawn down to its signs.",
  "greek.iamb.3.18":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question whether a god, an angel, a daemon or some other power is present in manifestations, divinations and other sacred acts. Nothing in divine works can be done rightly without one of the higher kinds overseeing and completing it: the gods lead the works that are perfect and self-sufficient, angels complete and reveal those in the middle, and daemons carry out the lowest. Since one cannot speak rightly of the gods without the gods, still less can anyone do works of divine rank or foreknow all things without them. The human race is weak and of little worth, sees little and has a nothingness born in it; the only cure for its error, confusion and instability is to share as far as it can in divine light. Whoever leaves that out is like someone trying to make soul from the lifeless or mind from the mindless. But it does not follow, as Porphyry assumes, that these powers act because they are drawn through us by the 'necessities' of invocation. The divine, and the whole choir of higher beings that hangs from it, is above necessity, both the necessity men bring and the necessity that holds the world. Invocation and the acts of the skilled operator reach the higher beings through likeness and kinship, not by force; what is seen in diviners does not happen because the theurgist is acted on, nor is divination produced by necessity or by passion seizing the one who foretells.",
  "greek.iamb.3.19":
    "Abammon answers that the cause of the higher beings' works is not a go-between, nor does the invoker work through the prophet; to say so is impious. It is truer to say that God is all things, can do all things, fills all things with himself and alone deserves our care, esteem, thought and honour, while what is human is worthless and laughable beside the divine. He laughs to hear it said that the divine comes of its own accord to certain people or things because of the cycle of birth or other causes: then the unborn would be led by the cycle of birth and would no longer be the first cause of all. Such views are unworthy of the gods and foreign to theurgy. They suffer the same fault as the many do about the making of the world and providence: unable to learn how these work, and refusing to give the gods human cares and reasonings, they abolish providence altogether. As one answers them that the divine way of making and caring differs from the human way and must not be denied out of ignorance, so one may answer Porphyry that all foretelling and all divine works are the works of the gods, done through causes known only to them, not through human ones.",
  "greek.iamb.3.20":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's second suggested cause, that 'the soul says and imagines these things, and they are its passions, stirred by small causes'. Neither nature nor reason allows this. Everything comes from a cause, and like from like; a divine work is neither chance, which has no cause, nor the product of a human cause, which is lower, for the more perfect cannot come from the less. The human soul is held to one form and darkened on every side by the body, which one may call the river of Neglect, the water of Forgetfulness, ignorance and madness, a bond of passions or the loss of life without saying enough of its baseness. How could a soul held by so many evils be equal to such work? If we ever seem able to do it, it is only by sharing in and being lit by the gods. The soul does not take part in divine works through its own virtue and wisdom; otherwise every soul, or at least every perfect soul, would do them, whereas even the perfect soul falls short of divine activity. The theurgic work is something else, and success in divine works is given by the gods alone; if it were not, there would be no need to worship the gods, and divine goods would come to us from ourselves without religion. This opinion, foolish as it is, must be given up.",
  "greek.iamb.3.21":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's third suggestion, that divination is 'a mixed kind of being made from our soul and divine inspiration coming from outside'. Wherever one thing is made from two, it is of one kind, nature and essence, as elements combine into one thing or many souls into one whole soul. But what is wholly above cannot become one with what falls short of itself: if the divine is unmixed, the soul cannot be mixed with it, and if it is unchanging, it cannot be changed into something shared. Some earlier thinkers said that small sparks wake divine forms in us, but sparks that are natural or bodily cannot pass from chance things to divine ones. Porphyry's view makes the soul a joint cause of the divine mixture, so that the soul would be equal to the gods, giving them a part and taking a part, measuring natures higher than itself and being bounded by them. It would follow, worst of all, that gods placed among the elements would be inside their effects, and something produced in time would contain the gods. If the mixture is both soul and inspiration, it is not one thing but a heap of two; if it is something different from both, eternal natures become changeable and divine ones no different from natural things that come to be. The opinion is unreasonable and paradoxical either way.",
  "greek.iamb.3.22":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's suggestion that the soul produces a power that imagines the future through such motions, or that things taken from matter, especially from animals, make daemons through the powers in them. This, he says, is a dreadful offence against all theology and theurgy. It makes daemons born and perishable; worse, it makes earlier things come from later ones, for daemons exist before the soul and before the powers spread through bodies. How could the activities of a divided soul held in a body become a being separate from soul, or powers whose being is in bodies be separated from them, and who would gather what has been dissolved into one? Such a daemon would exist before it was made. And how could divination come from what has no power to divine, soul from the soulless, or the more perfect from the less? Being cannot come from what has no being. Nor can the imagination receive more than its first cause gives it. Someone might say that daemons preside over matter taken from animals and are moved by sympathy when it is brought; then daemons are not made from bodily powers but exist before bodies. Even granting such sympathy, foreknowledge of the future does not belong to a power that suffers with matter and is held in one place and body, but to one free of them all.",
  "greek.iamb.3.23":
    "Abammon answers the next objections, which begin by doubting how divination works and end by trying to overthrow it. First, Porphyry's remark that 'in sleep, when we are doing nothing, we sometimes learn the future'. This does not mean that the cause of divination comes partly from us and partly from outside. Where a thing begins in us and the rest follows from outside, the outcome is fixed by both. But where the cause does not depend on us and exists beforehand by itself, the end is not set by us, and everything depends on what is outside us. Since the truth in dreams does not wholly match what we do, but often shines out of itself, it shows that divination comes from the gods outside us, has an independent power, and kindly reveals the future when and how it wishes.",
  "greek.iamb.3.24":
    "Abammon answers that in trying to explain divination Porphyry overthrows it. If a passion of the soul were its cause, what wise man would credit an unstable and senseless thing with orderly, steady foreknowledge? How could the soul be ignorant of the future when its higher, intellectual and reasoning powers are sound, but know it when it is shaken by disorderly motions? Passion hinders true understanding rather than helping it. If the things in the world were made by passions, passions might be akin to them; but they are made through principles and forms, so their foreknowledge must be free of passion. Passion perceives only what is present now, while foreknowledge grasps what does not yet exist. As for Porphyry's arguments: that 'the senses are occupied' shows that no human image is then aroused; the 'fumigations' are akin to the god, not to the soul of the onlooker; the 'invocations' do not stir reasoning or bodily passions, being unknown and secret, known only to the god they call; and that 'not all, but the simpler and the young are more apt for divination' shows that such people are better prepared to receive the inspiring spirit that comes from outside. These signs show that inspiration flows in from outside, not that it is a passion.",
  "greek.iamb.3.25":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's claim that 'the madness that comes with diseases is the cause of divination', likening inspiration to an excess of black bile, drunkenness and the frenzy from a mad dog's bite. There are two kinds of ecstasy. One leads to a worse state and fills us with folly; the other gives goods more honourable than human self-control. One strays into disorderly, material motion and drags the soul down; the other gives itself to the cause that orders the world, is above nature, raises the soul and joins us to the divine. What has inspiration in common with melancholy, drunkenness or bodily delirium, or what foretelling ever came from disease? Bad 'enthusiasm' comes from weakness, the better from fullness of power: in the better the soul rests and lets a higher power use it. So divine madness must be cleared of all human derangement; the divine 'sobriety and wakefulness' is not human sobriety; the images of disease are not divine images; and images produced by enchantment have neither the being nor the truth of what is really seen. These objections, raised in a spirit of contention rather than inquiry, he will not discuss further.",
  "greek.iamb.3.26":
    "Abammon answers that it is astonishing to place the truth of divination either with enchanters, whose work is mere appearance, or with those driven by passion or disease, whose bold utterances are deceit; what principle of truth or help to understanding can such madmen have? Chance truths, such as may come to those who rush about at random, do not count; nor does the kind of truth that arises when what acts and what is acted on happen to match, which animals have in their senses and imaginations and which is nothing divine. The truth of divination is steady in its working, has the whole knowledge of beings present to it, is one with the being of things, uses stable principles, and knows all things fully and definitely. So it is far from a natural presentiment, like some animals' sense of earthquakes or rain, which comes from sympathy with parts of the universe or from a keen sense that perceives changes in the air before they reach the ground. Such natural impressions resemble foreknowledge but fall short of it in stability and truth, dealing with what happens often but not always. Even arts that foresee the future, such as piloting or medicine, guess from signs that are not always reliable or always tied to what they signify; but divine providence has steady knowledge of the future, an unshakeable trust hanging from causes, a grasp of all things in all, and a lasting knowledge of everything as present.",
  "greek.iamb.3.27":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's claim that 'nature, art and the sympathy of things in the universe, as if they were parts of one living being, foreshow some things by others'. These show a scattered trace of divine prediction, since a faint or clear image of it shines in all things, as an image of the good does. But none of them is divine divination, which is not to be judged by its images in the world of birth but grasped as one unchanging, intelligible truth. If divination is a divine work, it cannot be ascribed to nature, which works without reason and mind, as though nature had built a prophetic apparatus into us. Nature prepares aptitudes only for the perfections that belong to us as human beings, not for a good older than our nature, so there is no natural seed of divine prediction in us. There may be a human kind of divination with a natural basis, varying by more and less, but it is separate from the divine kind. Porphyry's own examples, that those who invoke the gods carry stones and herbs, tie and untie sacred knots and open what is shut, show that the inspiration comes from outside.",
  "greek.iamb.3.28":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's mention of 'the makers of effective images' as not to be despised. No theurgist who sees the true forms of the gods would accept them: why exchange true beings for images and go down from the first to the last of things? What such work produces is a phantom of the true, seeming good but not being so. A man makes these images, not a god, from matter taken for the purpose, and what comes from human art is weaker than its makers. God makes the worlds not through the motions of the heavens or divided matter, but by thoughts, will and immaterial forms, through an eternal soul above the world. The image-maker is said to work through the revolving stars, but he does not. The last of the heavenly gods' countless powers is the natural power, which rules visible coming-to-be about the earth and is used by medicine and other arts too. The image-maker cannot touch the heavenly circuits; he applies himself by art, not theurgy, to the last outflows of nature at the lowest part of the universe, mixed with matter and easily changed. Such a compound of material powers is foreign to divine making and even to nature; it is merely artificial.",
  "greek.iamb.3.29":
    "Abammon answers by asking why the image-maker, who is better than his images, abandons himself and trusts in lifeless idols that have only a show of life, a patched-up harmony, a varied outward form, and last a day. Nothing made by human art is genuine and pure. The idol is not simple and uniform in its working, but mixed from all kinds of opposite qualities; it has no pure, perfect power, only a borrowed crowd of outflows gathered from many places, weak and fading. Nor is it stable, as its defenders claim: idols vanish faster than images in mirrors, for they are formed by the fumes of incense, and as soon as the smoke spreads through the air the idol dissolves and cannot last a moment. Why should a lover of truth bother with such useless illusions? If their makers know that these are only shapes of passive matter, the harm is simple; but the image-makers become like the images they trust, and if they treat them as gods the absurdity cannot be spoken or borne. No divine splendour shines on such a soul, for it cannot be given to what is wholly opposed to it, and those held by dark phantoms have no room to receive it. This making of phantoms deals in shadows far from the truth.",
  "greek.iamb.3.30":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's statement that the image-makers watch the motions of the heavenly bodies and can tell from the meeting of one star with others whether predictions will be true or false, and whether their works will be empty or meaningful and effective. That gives the phantoms nothing divine: the lowest things in the world of birth move with the heavenly courses and are affected by their outflows. Looked at closely, it shows the opposite of what is claimed: how could things so easily changed, turned this way and that by outside motions to be idle or prophetic, meaningful or effective, hold any share of divine power? Nor are the powers in matter the building blocks of daemons: particular sensible bodies do not produce daemons, but are produced and guarded by them, and no one can fashion forms of daemons as with a machine; rather a man, so far as he has a sensible body, is fashioned by them. The daemonic host is simple and works uniformly upon composite things, and gives sensible things what permanence they can take. Unless one calls idols daemons, wrongly: the nature and order of daemons and of idols are quite different, and the leader of idols is other than the great leader of daemons. Porphyry himself admits that 'no god or daemon is drawn down by idols'. What then is a sacred act or a foreknowledge worth that is wholly without god or daemon? One should know what this wonder-working art is, but never use it or trust it.",
  "greek.iamb.3.31":
    "Abammon answers the still worse explanation that divination is caused by 'a kind of daemons that is by nature deceitful', pretending to be gods, daemons and the souls of the dead. He tells what he once heard from the prophets of the Chaldeans. The true gods alone give good and keep company only with good men purified by the priestly art; when they shine, what is evil vanishes like darkness before light and cannot disturb theurgists. But the wicked, who leap at divine things lawlessly, are shut out from pure spirits by their pollutions, join evil spirits, are filled with the worst inspiration and grow like the evil daemons they are akin to. Such impious practices, which bring evil daemons, 'counter-gods', in place of gods, have no place in a discussion of priestly divination; every evil spirit flees from theurgists. True divination is pure, priestly and divine, above nature and eternal. Porphyry and every lover of the gods should give himself wholly to it, for through it come unerring truth and perfect virtue, by which theurgists ascend to the intelligible fire, the end of all foreknowledge. Those who say divination comes from an evil daemon, raised in darkness, cannot tell truth from falsehood.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- Section IV
  "greek.iamb.4.1":
    "Abammon answers by turning to the objections and asks Porphyry to bear with him if he speaks at length, since the greatest subjects need long and careful study. Porphyry's doubt is that 'it greatly puzzles me how higher beings, when invoked, are commanded by those who invoke them, as if they were inferior'. Abammon sets out the powers that are invoked. The gods and the beings above us, out of love of the beautiful and an overflowing fullness of good, kindly give the worthy what suits them, pitying the labours of the priests and delighting in those they have begotten, nourished and taught. The middle kinds are the watchful guardians of judgement: they tell us what to do and what to avoid, help just works and hinder unjust ones, and make those who rob or harm others suffer the same in return. There is also a wholly irrational kind of daemons, without judgement, each given one power and set over one task only; as a sword can only cut, so of the spirits spread through the universe by the divided necessity of nature, one kind separates things that come to be and another gathers them. This can be seen in the so-called Charonian vents, which give off a vapour that destroys whatever falls into them. Whoever turns such things from their natural course and uses them unlawfully suffers the harm of what he misuses; but that belongs to another discussion.",
  "greek.iamb.4.2":
    "Abammon answers that it does sometimes happen that spirits are given commands, spirits that have no reason or judgement of their own, and not without reason. Our mind, which can reason about and judge things as they are and holds many powers of life, is used to commanding the most irrational spirits, which are perfected by one activity alone. It invokes them as higher, because it tries to draw from the whole world things that serve the whole; and it commands them as lower, because a part, such as our reasoning power, is often purer and more perfect than things spread through the whole world, as what has intellect has more authority than what is lifeless, however much greater in extent. Another reason is that in all theurgic work the priest has a double character: as a man, he keeps the rank our nature has in the universe; but strengthened by divine signs and joined through them to higher natures, he is raised to their order and can rightly put on the outward form of the gods. So, as a man, he invokes the powers of the universe as higher; and, clothed by secret symbols in the sacred form of the gods, he commands them.",
  "greek.iamb.4.3":
    "Abammon answers with a truer solution: in invoking the higher natures, the calls that seem to be made to them as to men, and the urgent commands given in the work, should be set aside. If the bonds of priestly work are a shared, harmonious friendship and an unbreakable union, so that the work is truly divine and above every human action, then nothing human fits it; the priest's invocations are not like drawing distant things to us, nor are his commands given to things apart from him. The activity of the divine fire shines out of its own will and in common, calling and moving itself, and works the same way through everything, both what gives its light and what receives it. This solution is far better, for it holds that divine works are done not through opposition and difference, as things are produced in the world of birth, but through sameness, union and agreement. If invoker and invoked, commander and commanded, higher and lower are set against each other, the conflicts of the world of birth are carried into the unborn goods of the gods. If all that is dismissed as earthbound, and what is shared and simple is given to the powers above the variety of birth, Porphyry's first question falls at once.",
  "greek.iamb.4.4":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's next question, why the powers invoked require their worshipper to be just, yet do not refuse to act unjustly when asked. He doubts what Porphyry calls acting justly, and thinks that what seems to us an exact definition of justice does not seem so to the gods. We look at what is shortest, at present things and this brief life; the higher powers know the whole life of the soul and all its former lives. So if they inflict a punishment at the prayer of those who invoke them, they do not do it unjustly, but with an eye to the offences the soul committed in earlier lives, which men do not see, and so think they suffer their misfortunes unjustly.",
  "greek.iamb.4.5":
    "Abammon answers that people commonly raise the same doubt about providence: why some suffer undeservedly, having done nothing wrong before. Here too we cannot know what the soul is and its whole life, how many wrongs it did in former lives, and whether it now suffers for them; and many wrongs in this life are hidden from men but known to the gods. Nor do the gods aim at the same justice as men. Men define justice as the soul doing its own work and giving each his due under the laws and constitution in force; the gods judge what is just by looking at the whole order of the world and the relation of souls to the gods. It is no wonder that we cannot reach their highest judgement in most things. Nothing stops the gods from decreeing justice for each soul by itself and also together with the whole kinship of souls; for if souls, in and out of bodies, share one nature and one common order with the life of the world, justice must be fulfilled for the whole, especially when one soul's past wrongs are greater than a single punishment can pay. These rules are enough to show the general kind of the healing punishments that divine justice inflicts.",
  "greek.iamb.4.6":
    "Abammon answers that, to meet the objection with more arguments, he will grant for the sake of argument the opposite of what he has said, that some unjust things are done in the business of invocations. Even so, the gods are plainly not to blame: the good cause good, and the gods are good by essence, so they do nothing unjust, and other causes of wrongdoing must be sought. If we cannot find them, we should not throw away the true idea of the gods, or abandon clear notions of them because of doubts about how such wrongs are done. It is far better to admit that we cannot explain how unjust acts are done than to accept anything impossible and false about the gods, for all Greeks and barbarians rightly believe the opposite of the divine.",
  "greek.iamb.4.7":
    "Abammon answers by adding the causes from which evils sometimes arise, which are many and varied. If what he said about images and evil daemons who take on the look of gods and good daemons is true, a large evil-working tribe appears from which such contradictions flow. An evil daemon demands that his worshipper be just, because he pretends to be of the divine kind, but serves injustice, because he is wicked. As with good and evil, so with true and false: in divination true predictions are given to the gods alone and any falsehood to another kind of cause, that of daemons; so in matters of justice, what is fine and just is to be given only to the gods and good daemons, and what is unjust and base to daemons who are wicked by nature. What agrees with itself and is always the same belongs to the higher natures; what is at odds with itself and never the same belongs above all to daemonic strife, in which it is no wonder that contradictions arise; it would be more surprising if they did not.",
  "greek.iamb.4.8":
    "Abammon answers by proving the same from another starting point. The bodily parts of the universe are not idle or powerless; their power is as much greater than ours as they surpass us in perfection, beauty and size. Each by itself can do various things, and they do still more upon each other, and many kinds of effect pass from wholes to parts, partly by sympathy through likeness of powers and partly by the fitness of agent to patient. If evils and destruction befall parts, they are healthy and good for the wholes and the harmony of the universe, but bring necessary decay to the parts, either because the parts cannot bear the activity of the wholes, or because of some mixture with their own weakness, or because the parts are out of proportion with one another.",
  "greek.iamb.4.9":
    "Abammon answers that many things also come about from the nature of the universe's body. The agreement of like things and the opposition of unlike things do much; and the gathering of many things into the one living being of the universe, and the powers in the world, produce one effect in wholes and another in parts, because of the parts' divided weakness. So the friendship, love and strife that are activities in the universe become passions in the single beings that share in them; what is laid down in forms and pure principles in the whole takes on a material lack and shapelessness in the parts; and what is joined in the whole is separated in the parts. Divided things, sharing in wholes together with matter, fall short of them in everything, in beauty and perfection too. Some parts perish so that wholes may be kept in their natural state, and sometimes parts are crushed and weighed down while the wholes feel no such trouble.",
  "greek.iamb.4.10":
    "Abammon answers by drawing his conclusions. If some who invoke make use of the natural or bodily powers of the universe, these powers give their activity without choosing and without fault, but the user sometimes turns the gift to its opposite and to base ends. The gift moves with the passions and by sympathy through likeness, but the user deliberately and unjustly draws it to what is evil and base. The gift makes even the most distant things work together through the one harmony of the world; if someone who knows this wickedly tries to draw some parts of the universe towards others, the parts are not to blame for the evil, but human daring and the breaking of the world's order, which twist what is fine and lawful. So the gods do not do what seems base; it is done through the natures and bodies that come from them, and even these do not give wickedness of themselves, but send their outflows to the earth for the preservation of the whole, which those who receive them change by mixing and twisting them to a purpose other than that for which they were given. So the divine nature is not the cause of evils and unjust deeds.",
  "greek.iamb.4.11":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's doubt how the gods refuse to hear one who invokes them while impure from sexual intercourse, yet do not refuse to lead anyone into unlawful love. What has been said already gives a clear solution: such things may be against human laws but happen by another order and cause higher than laws; or they may agree with the harmony and friendship of the world while causing conflict among parts through a kind of sympathy; or a good that is rightly given may be twisted to its opposite by those who receive it.",
  "greek.iamb.4.12":
    "Abammon answers by explaining this in detail. The universe is one living being, whose parts are separated by place but hasten towards each other because they share one nature. The whole power and cause of mixture draws the parts to mingle of its own accord, but art can stir and stretch this attraction further than is fitting. The cause of mixture, reaching through the whole world, is good, brings fullness, creates fellowship, agreement and proportion, and plants in union the unbreakable principle of love, which holds and keeps both what exists and what is coming to be. But in the parts, separated from each other and from the whole and by nature imperfect, needy and weak, the bond is joined with passion, and desire and appetite are born in most of them. Art sees this desire planted by nature and draws it off in various ways, as through a channel; so it turns what is ordered into disorder, fills what is fine and proportioned with ugliness, turns the noble end that goes with union into an unseemly fullness gathered from a shared passion, brings in matter unfit for the making of what is beautiful, and mixes many natural powers as it likes. So the apparatus of such sexual union comes from a human art, not from any daemonic or divine necessity.",
  "greek.iamb.4.13":
    "Abammon answers by pointing to another kind of cause: how a stone or a herb often has by itself a power to destroy or to bind together things that come to be. This natural power is found in greater things too, and those who cannot reason it out may credit the works of nature to higher beings, gods, angels and daemons. It is agreed that the tribe of evil daemons has very wide power over the world of birth, human affairs and things on earth; so it is no wonder that such a tribe does such works. Not everyone can tell a good daemon from an evil one, and those who cannot reason wrongly about the cause and refer it to kinds above nature and above daemons. If powers of a particular soul are used for these things, whether the soul is still in a body or has left its shell-like, earthy body and wanders about the places of birth in a murky, moist spirit, that would be true, but it puts the cause as far as possible from the higher natures. So nothing divine, and no good daemon, serves men's unlawful sexual desires; there are many other causes of such things.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- Section V
  "greek.iamb.5.1":
    "Abammon answers by restating Porphyry's next doubt, one shared by learned and unlearned alike, about sacrifices: 'what use or power they have in the universe and with the gods, and why they are offered, fittingly to the powers honoured, and usefully to those who give the gifts'. With it comes another objection: that the interpreters of prophecies and oracles must abstain from animals so that the gods are not polluted by their vapours, which contradicts the claim that the gods are especially drawn by the vapours of animals.",
  "greek.iamb.5.2":
    "Abammon answers that the contradiction is easily resolved by showing how wholes rank above parts and how far the gods are above men. The soul of the whole presides over the entire body of the world, and the heavenly gods mount into a heavenly body as into a vehicle without harm or hindrance to their thought; but for a particular soul, sharing a body is harmful in both ways. If someone still argues that if the body is a bond to our soul it must be a bond to the world-soul, and if a particular soul turns towards the body for the sake of birth so must the gods' power, anyone may answer that he does not know how far higher beings surpass men, and wholes parts. Since the objections concern different things, they create no real difficulty.",
  "greek.iamb.5.3":
    "Abammon answers that the same reasoning suffices here. In us, the enjoyment of bodies that were once joined to a soul leaves heaviness and pollution, breeds sensuality and causes many other diseases of the soul. But with the gods and with the universal causes of the world this is not so: the vapour that rises in a divine way from sacrificed animals is held by the gods and does not hold them, and is joined to the universe without binding wholes and the gods to itself; so it is fitted to the higher beings and universal causes, but does not restrain them or fit them to itself.",
  "greek.iamb.5.4":
    "Abammon answers that abstinence from animals, which troubles Porphyry, is no difficulty when rightly understood. Worshippers do not abstain lest the gods be polluted by vapours, for what vapour can come near beings who cut away matter before anything material can touch them? Even a heavenly body takes nothing foreign into itself. How could an earthly vapour, which does not rise five stades from the ground, feed or pollute a circling, immaterial body that has no opposite and is free from change? What is unborn cannot be changed by what is born. It is not the gods who can be polluted by vapours, but we: things that are divided, material and akin can act on one another, but things wholly different and above cannot. Pollution by matter falls on those held in a material body, and they are the ones who need purifying. The divine, having nothing in common with us, cannot be polluted by our passions. So the supposed contradiction falls; Porphyry's doubts would not fit even good men, for no sensible man would be lured by the smell of vapours, still less any higher being.",
  "greek.iamb.5.5":
    "Abammon answers that Porphyry's next question is weightier and harder, and needs a long answer, but he will give it willingly, following what Porphyry has briefly hinted. His own teaching on sacrifices is this: they must not be offered only for the sake of honour, as we honour benefactors, nor only to give thanks for the goods the gods have given us, nor only as first-fruits or a return gift for the higher goods they have bestowed. These reasons are shared with human dealings and taken from ordinary social life, and do not respect the gods' surpassing rank and their order as causes set above all.",
  "greek.iamb.5.6":
    "Abammon answers that the most important thing about sacrifices is their effective power, and why they are so beneficial that without them we are not freed from plague, famine or barren crops, nor get rain in season, nor, more important still, purification of the soul or release from the cycle of birth. The reasons Porphyry gives for sacrifice do not explain any of this, and so cannot rightly be accepted, since they give a cause unworthy of what sacrifices do; if they are accepted at all, it can only be as secondary reasons depending on first, older and more venerable causes.",
  "greek.iamb.5.7":
    "Abammon answers that he must show how sacrifices take effect and depend on the gods, the first causes of their effects. One might say that the fellowship of like powers, the opposition of contraries, or the fitness of agent to patient in the universe, as in one living being with one life everywhere, stirs up like things together by one sympathy, so that the most distant things are nearest. That says something true, which necessarily goes along with sacrifices, but it does not show how they really work. For the essence of the gods does not lie in nature and natural necessity, to be stirred by natural passions or by the powers that run through nature; it is defined by itself, independently, and has nothing in common with them in essence, power or anything else.",
  "greek.iamb.5.8":
    "Abammon answers that the same absurdities follow from giving as the causes of sacrifices' effects certain numbers among us, such as the number sixty in the crocodile, as suited to the sun; or natural principles such as the powers and activities of animals, the dog, the dog-headed baboon and the weasel, which are shared with the moon; or material forms seen in sacred animals, their colours and bodily shapes; or anything else in the bodies of the animals or other things offered, or a single organ such as the heart of a cock. Such causes do not show the gods to be above nature, nor stirred as such by sacrifices; they make them natural causes held by matter, wrapped up in bodies, stirred and stilled with them. If anything of the kind is involved in sacrifices, it is a helping cause, something without which the thing is not done, and depends on prior causes.",
  "greek.iamb.5.9":
    "Abammon answers that it is better to give as the cause of sacrifices' power friendship and kinship, the bond that ties makers to what they make and begetters to what they beget. When, with this shared principle in place, we take an animal or something that grows from the earth which keeps the will of its maker genuinely and purely, through it we fittingly move the creative cause that presides over it without being stained. These causes are many: some, the daemonic, are close to us; others, the divine, are ranked above them; and one most ancient and venerable cause leads them all. A perfect sacrifice moves all the causes together, each thing being fitted to it according to its rank; an imperfect sacrifice goes a certain distance and no further. So many hold that sacrifices are to be offered to good daemons, many to the last powers of the gods, many to the worldly or earthly powers of daemons or gods. These are not false as parts of sacrifice, but they do not take in its whole power and all its goods, which reach everything divine.",
  "greek.iamb.5.10":
    "Abammon answers that he accepts these views: natural beings are stirred together as in one living being by sympathy, and daemons and the worldly divine powers are the first to be made familiar to us. But the most perfect cause of sacrifice's power is joined to the creative powers, which hold all its causes, and from them a shared benefit is given to cities, peoples, houses or a single person, without passion, through one friendship that holds all things together. This is far truer than Porphyry's suspicion that 'the gods are especially drawn by the vapours of animal sacrifices'. If daemons have a body, it is unchanging, impassive, luminous and in need of nothing. Even if it needed inflow, the air has an unfailing supply of vapours from the earth, so what use would sacrifices be? Nor would the maker of the universe have given all animals easy food but made the beings above us depend on food from men, so that if we grew lazy the daemons would starve. That would make us the higher kind, since everything is fed by what produced it, as the earth by the heavens and the soul by intellect. As we are not the causes of daemons, we cannot be the causes of their food.",
  "greek.iamb.5.11":
    "Abammon answers that the question goes wrong in another way: it does not see that offering sacrifices by fire destroys and consumes their matter, makes the matter like the fire rather than the fire like the matter, and lifts up to divine, heavenly and immaterial fire rather than sinking down to matter and birth. If the enjoyment of material vapours drew daemons, the matter would need to stay pure and whole so as to give off more; but in fact it is all burned up and changed into the purity and fineness of fire, which plainly shows the opposite of what Porphyry says. The higher beings are impassive, delight in cutting away matter by fire, and make us impassive; they make what is in us like the gods, as fire turns solid, resisting things into bright and fine ones, and through sacrifices and the sacrificial fire they raise us to the fire of the gods, as fire raises fire and draws heavy things up towards the divine and heavenly.",
  "greek.iamb.5.12":
    "Abammon answers that the vehicle that serves daemons is not made of matter, nor of the elements, nor of any body we know. What food can pass from one kind of being to another of a different kind, or what enjoyment come from the foreign to the foreign? None. Rather, as the gods by the fire of lightning divide matter and separate from it what is by essence immaterial but held and bound by it, and make the passive impassive, so our fire, imitating the divine fire, destroys everything material in sacrifices, purifies what is offered, frees it from the bonds of matter and makes it fit by purity for fellowship with the gods. In the same way it frees us from the bonds of birth, makes us like the gods, fits us for their friendship and leads our material nature to an immaterial being.",
  "greek.iamb.5.13":
    "Abammon answers that, having overturned the common absurd opinions about sacrifices, he will put true ideas in their place. He will not discuss each kind of sacrifice separately, since that belongs to another inquiry and an intelligent reader can work it out from what has been said, reasoning from one case to many. He thinks the matter sufficiently explained, especially as the explanation respects the purity of the gods; but because others may find it hard to believe, not clear enough, or not convincing to reason, he will consider it a little more fully and, if possible, add clearer arguments.",
  "greek.iamb.5.14":
    "Abammon answers by showing that the sacred law of sacrifices is bound up with the order of the gods. Some gods are material and some immaterial: the material gods hold matter within themselves and order it; the immaterial are wholly above it. In the sacrificial art one must begin the rites with the material gods, for otherwise there is no ascent to the immaterial ones. The material gods share in matter so far as they preside over it, and rule what happens in matter: the division, striking and rebounding, change, birth and decay of all material bodies. Whoever wishes to worship them theurgically, as suits them and their domain, should use a material kind of worship, since they are material; so we are led into kinship with them and worship them fittingly. Dead bodies and lifeless things, the slaughter of animals, the burning of victims and the change of the matter offered belong to these gods, not for their own sake but because of the matter they rule; for though they are wholly separate from it, they are present to it, and what is governed is not foreign to its governors, nor the instrument to its user. So it is unfitting to offer matter in sacrifice to the immaterial gods, but most fitting to all the material gods.",
  "greek.iamb.5.15":
    "Abammon answers by turning to our own double condition. There is a time when we become wholly soul, out of the body, and circle on high with all the immaterial gods; and a time when we are bound in the shell-like body, held by matter, and bodily in nature. So there are two kinds of worship: one simple, bodiless and pure of all birth, which belongs to unstained souls; the other full of bodies and material things, which suits souls not yet pure or free of birth. There are likewise two kinds of sacrifice: one for wholly purified men, which, as Heraclitus says, rarely happens to one man or to a few easily counted; the other material and bodily, using change, suited to souls still held by the body. If cities and peoples not yet freed from the fate of birth and the hindering fellowship of bodies were not allowed this second kind, they would miss both immaterial and material good, being unable to receive the first and not offering what suits the second. Everyone sacrifices according to what he is, not what he is not, and the sacrifice should not exceed the measure of the one who offers it. The bond between worshippers and worshipped also calls for a matching worship: an immaterial bond for an immaterial worship joining bodiless natures by pure bodiless powers, and a bodily bond for a bodily worship that depends on bodies and the beings set over them.",
  "greek.iamb.5.16":
    "Abammon answers that we must not disdain to add that we often do things for the gods who watch over the body and for good daemons for the body's necessary needs: when, by sacrifices, we purify it of old stains, free it from diseases and fill it with health, take away heaviness and sluggishness, or obtain some other good for it. In that case we plainly must not treat the body in an intellectual, bodiless way, for it cannot share in such ways; it is improved and purified by bodies, by things akin to it. So for such a purpose the sacrificial rites must be bodily, partly cutting away what is excessive in us, partly supplying what we lack, and partly bringing into proportion and order what is disturbed beyond measure. We also often perform rites asking the higher beings for what human life needs, such as keeping the body healthy, or for things we acquire for the body's sake.",
  "greek.iamb.5.17":
    "Abammon answers by asking what we could obtain from the gods who are wholly beyond human birth about barren years, plenty or anything else of mortal life. Nothing: it is not for beings free of all things to deal in such gifts. Someone may say that the wholly immaterial gods contain the material gods within them, and so contain their gifts under one first cause, and that in this way an abundance of divine gifts comes down from the immaterial gods. But no one may say that the immaterial gods give these gifts by stepping in directly to the affairs of human life, for such management is divided, involves turning towards what it cares for, is not wholly separate from bodies, and cannot hold a pure, unstained rule. So for such works the kind of sacrifice that is mixed with bodies and bound up with birth is most fitting, not the wholly immaterial and bodiless kind, which is far above and out of proportion with our affairs. The kind that uses bodies and the powers that work through bodies is most akin to human affairs, and can bring prosperity and give proportion and balance to the mortal race.",
  "greek.iamb.5.18":
    "Abammon answers with another division. The great mass of men are ranged under nature, governed by natural powers, look down to the works of nature, carry out the government of Fate, being of its order, and reason practically only about natural things. A certain few, using a power of intellect above nature, are removed from nature and led to a separate, unmixed intellect, and so rise above natural powers. Others, in between, tend towards what lies between nature and pure intellect: some follow both equally, some live a life mixed from both, and some free themselves from the lower and turn to the higher. From this it follows that those governed by the nature of the universe, living by it and using its powers, should adopt a worship suited to nature and to bodies moved by nature, choosing fitting places, air, matter and its powers, bodies and their states, qualities, motions and the changes of things that come to be, in sacrifice as in other parts of piety. Those who live by intellect alone and are freed from the bonds of nature should practise the intellectual, bodiless worship in every part of theurgy. And those in between should labour in the ways of piety according to their middle state, practising both kinds, or separating from one, or taking both as the foundation of higher things, without which they can never reach what is supreme.",
  "greek.iamb.5.19":
    "Abammon answers with a further division, of the divine essences and powers themselves. Some have soul and nature, the soul and nature of birth, under them and serving their works whenever they wish. Others are wholly separate from soul and nature, not only from the soul and nature of the world and of birth but even from a divine soul and nature. And others are in between, giving the extremes fellowship with each other, by an abundant share of greater good, an unhindered reception of lesser good, or a harmony that binds both. When we worship the gods who rule over soul and nature, it is not foreign to them to offer natural powers and bodies governed by nature, for all nature's works serve them. When we honour the gods who are one in essence, we must venerate them with freed honours: intellectual gifts, things that belong to a bodiless life, the fruits of virtue and wisdom and all the perfect and whole goods of the soul. To the gods in between, who lead the goods of the middle kind, sometimes double gifts are fitting, sometimes gifts that share in both, sometimes gifts set apart from the lower and belonging to the higher, or gifts that in some way complete the middle.",
  "greek.iamb.5.20":
    "Abammon answers by starting from another principle: the world and the gods of the world, the arrangement of the four elements, their association in due measures, and the ordered circling of bodies about centres. From there the truth about piety in sacrifices is easily reached. If we are in the world, are contained in the universe as parts, are first produced by it and perfected by its universal powers, are made of its elements and receive from it some share of life and nature, we should not leap beyond the world and its orders. In each part of the world there is this visible body, and there are bodiless powers divided about bodies; so the law of religion gives like to like, reaching from above through wholes to the last things, giving bodiless things to the bodiless and bodies to bodies, in proportion to each. If some theurgist shares in the gods above the world, which is the rarest of all things, he will rise above bodies and matter in his worship, united to the gods by a power above the world. But what happens to one person with difficulty, late, at the end of his priestly life, ought not to be proclaimed as common to all men, nor to those beginning theurgy, nor to those halfway on; for these give a bodily kind of care to holiness.",
  "greek.iamb.5.21":
    "Abammon answers that all who love theurgic truth will agree that piety towards the divine must not be partial. Before the gods appear, all the powers ranked beneath them are stirred and go before them like a procession; whoever does not honour each of them fittingly goes away without a share in the gods, while whoever wins them all over completes the whole receptacle of the divine choir. So holiness should not be simple and made of a few things, but many-formed and harmonised with everything in the world. The multitude of powers stirred when the gods come down is known only to theurgists, from experience of the rites. They alone know what makes the sacrificial art perfect, and that leaving out even a few things overturns the whole work of religion, as in music the breaking of one string puts the whole out of tune. As those who leave one of the higher kinds unhonoured at the gods' visible comings suffer plain harm, so in sacrifices one must honour each power according to its rank; whoever leaves one out throws the whole work of piety into confusion, and does not merely receive the gods imperfectly but overturns all the ceremonies of religion.",
  "greek.iamb.5.22":
    "Abammon answers that the highest kind of sacrifice reaches the one first god who rules over all, but that this comes late in life and only to very few; the sacrificial law is laid down for those who still need legislation. For everyone else sacrifice has to be fitted into the whole order of the gods and powers, so that no rank is passed over on the way up.",
  "greek.iamb.5.23":
    "Abammon answers that the many-sided character of holy rites is what purifies and perfects. There is a pure and divine matter, given by the father and maker of the world, which the gods can receive; so the theurgic art brings together stones, herbs, animals and aromatics that are holy, perfect and godlike, and makes of them a fit receptacle for the gods. Such matter is not to be despised, since the gods themselves showed it in blessed visions.",
  "greek.iamb.5.24":
    "Abammon answers that the gods are shared out over places, nations and the parts of the world, and that each should be offered the things that belong to it and that it produces. That is why some animals in Egypt, and man too, are held sacred, and why some offerings are kept whole while others are consecrated by being broken down and returned to their first elements.",
  "greek.iamb.5.25":
    "Abammon answers that in worship the divine leads, with gods and angels ranged round it, and that a ruling power presides over each nation and each temple. A god oversees sacrifices made to gods, an angel those made to angels, and a daemon those made to daemons. Nothing unworthy should be offered, and every power should be given its own honour.",
  "greek.iamb.5.26":
    "Abammon answers that prayer is part of sacrifice and has three kinds: one that gathers and brings us into contact with the divine, one that binds us in agreeing communion with it, and the highest, the seal of ineffable union. Its three fruits, like golden apples of the Hesperides, are illumination, a shared working with the gods, and being filled with divine fire. Prayer goes before, between or after the sacrificial act and is the divine key that opens the inner sanctuary of the gods. The end of sacrifice is union with the maker of the world, and the whole priestly discipline must therefore be kept entire.",
  // ---- Section VI
  "greek.iamb.6.1":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question why the overseer of the rites must not touch dead bodies when the rites themselves often use dead animals. A human corpse has lost the image of divine life that was in it, and so defiles; the bodies of animals consecrated for sacrifice are a different matter. Keeping away from the dead suits worship of the gods who are free of matter, while invocation through animals belongs to the gods set over animals.",
  "greek.iamb.6.2":
    "Abammon answers that dead bodies defile men, who are held in matter, but cannot defile bodiless daemons, so there is no contradiction between avoiding them and using animals in the rites.",
  "greek.iamb.6.3":
    "Abammon answers about divination through sacred animals such as hawks: it is not the gods who are drawn in this way but divided daemons of a lower order, and the soul of the animal, once freed from its body, serves as a medium for them.",
  "greek.iamb.6.4":
    "Abammon answers that the human soul which uses this kind of divination contributes to it through sympathy with the animal, and that it foretells only small, everyday matters. He does not count it as true divination.",
  "greek.iamb.6.5":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's charge that diviners threaten the gods, saying they will burst the heavens, reveal the secrets of Isis, show what is hidden in the inner shrine, stop the sacred boat, or scatter the limbs of Osiris to Typhon. Such threats are not aimed at the gods of heaven, he says, but at a lower, irrational and divided kind of power that is moved by images and appearances.",
  "greek.iamb.6.6":
    "Abammon answers that the theurgist gives commands through secret signs as though he stood in the rank of the gods, showing the power of his union with them, not because he could really do what he threatens. The daemons who guard particular things cannot bear words spoken against what they guard.",
  "greek.iamb.6.7":
    "Abammon answers that these daemons are guardians of the mysteries, and that order is kept because the power of Osiris stays undefiled, the life-giving beauties of Isis do not come down into body, the course of the sun is never stopped and the secrets are never revealed. The Chaldeans use no threats at all; the Egyptians, who mix daemonic words with divine signs, sometimes do.",
  // ---- Section VII
  "greek.iamb.7.1":
    "Abammon answers that Egyptian theology imitates nature and the making activity of the gods by means of symbols, and that its symbols must be read as pointing to intellectual and divine things.",
  "greek.iamb.7.2":
    "Abammon answers by explaining some of those symbols. The mire stands for matter, for all that is bodily and nourishing, the river of generation, which the god transcends. The god sitting on the lotus signifies his transcendence, the lotus being circular and so akin to intellect. The god sailing in a ship shows the sun governing the world as a pilot governs a ship from the helm.",
  "greek.iamb.7.3":
    "Abammon answers about the saying that the sun changes its form every hour according to the signs of the zodiac: it is the things that receive the god that vary, while the god himself is one. The Egyptian prayers to the sun follow this teaching.",
  "greek.iamb.7.4":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's question about the power of names that seem to mean nothing. They are not meaningless: even if we do not know what they mean (though the gods have explained some of them), they all mean something to the gods, not in the way human words do but intellectually or ineffably. Their very unknowability is what is most venerable in them, and through them the soul keeps the hidden image of the gods and is lifted towards them. Foreign names are preferred to Greek ones because the gods have shown that the languages of sacred nations such as the Egyptians and Assyrians suit sacred things, because those languages are the first and most ancient, and because the sacred law of tradition should be kept unchanged in the tongue in which the names of the gods were first learned.",
  "greek.iamb.7.5":
    "Abammon answers the objection that only the meaning matters, whatever words are used. If names were mere convention that would be true, but names belong to the nature of things, so the ones most fitted to it are dearest to the gods. Meanings are not fully kept in translation, and even translated words lose their power; foreign names are also weightier, more concise and less ambiguous. The Egyptians were the first people given a share in the gods, so the gods delight in Egyptian rites. Ancient prayers must be kept unchanged, like sacred sanctuaries; names and prayers have lost their power because the Greeks, always eager for novelty, keep changing them, while the barbarians hold firmly to the same words and are therefore dear to the gods.",
  // ---- Section VIII
  "greek.iamb.8.1":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's questions about what the Egyptians take the first cause to be: whether it is intellect or above intellect, one or more, bodiless or bodily, the same as the maker of the world or prior to him; whether all things come from one principle or many; and whether matter is unbegotten. He first explains why the ancient sacred writers disagree: there are many different kinds of being, and different priests handed down different principles. According to Seleucus, Hermes described the universal principles in twenty thousand books; according to Manetho, in 36,525 volumes.",
  "greek.iamb.8.2":
    "Abammon answers that before all truly existing beings and universal principles there is one God, prior even to the first god and king, unmoved and abiding in the solitude of his own unity, the pattern of the self-begotten god, the fountain of all things and the root of the first intelligible forms. From this one the self-sufficient god unfolds himself into light: the principle and god of gods, a monad from the one, prior to being and the source of being. Hermes places these most ancient principles before the ethereal, empyrean and celestial gods, and wrote a hundred books on the empyrean gods, a hundred on the ethereal and a thousand on the celestial.",
  "greek.iamb.8.3":
    "Abammon answers by giving another Egyptian ordering. Hermes puts the god Emeph, an intellect thinking itself, before and at the head of the celestial gods, and before him the indivisible one called Eicton, the first pattern, worshipped only in silence. The demiurgic intellect who brings hidden reasons into light is called Amon in Egyptian, Ptah as the maker who works truly and with art (the Greeks equate Ptah with Hephaestus), and Osiris as the giver of good. Other powers rule the elements, four male and four female, assigned to the sun, and the whole of nature in generation, assigned to the moon; the heavens are divided into two, four, twelve or thirty-six parts with their rulers, and one ruler is set over all. So Egyptian teaching runs from one principle down to the many it governs. God produced matter by separating materiality from essentiality, and the maker of the world shaped from it the simple spheres and the bodies that come to be and pass away.",
  "greek.iamb.8.4":
    "Abammon answers that the books circulating under the name of Hermes contain Hermetic opinions even though they often use the language of the philosophers, because they were translated from Egyptian by men skilled in philosophy. Chaeremon and others wrote only about the lowest rulers, and writers on the planets, the zodiac, the decans and horoscopes about their divided distributions. The Egyptians do not say that everything is physical: they separate the life of soul and intellect from nature, in the universe and in us, and place a pure intellect above the world. Through priestly theurgy they say they can rise to the higher and more universal beings set above fate, to God and the maker of the world, using no matter but only the observation of the right time.",
  "greek.iamb.8.5":
    "Abammon answers that Hermes told of this god-making and upward path, and that Bitys, prophet of King Ammon, explained it, having found it written in hieroglyphs in the inner shrines of Sais, and also handed down the name of God that runs through the whole world. So Porphyry is wrong to reduce everything Egyptian to physical causes: the Egyptians recognise many principles and beings, and powers above the world, which they worship with priestly holiness.",
  "greek.iamb.8.6":
    "Abammon answers the claim that many Egyptians make what is in our power depend on the movement of the stars. According to the Hermetic writings, man has two souls: one comes from the first intelligible and shares in the power of the maker of the world, the other is given from the revolution of the heavens. The soul that comes down from the worlds follows their cycles, but the soul present from the intelligible rises above the motion of generation, and through it come freedom from fate and the ascent to the intelligible gods.",
  "greek.iamb.8.7":
    "Abammon answers that it is not true that everything is bound by the unbreakable bonds of necessity, which we call fate. The soul has its own principle for turning back to the intelligible and touching true being. The gods worshipped in temples and statues do free us from fate; it is the lowest powers that come from them, entangled with generation and body, that make up fate. There is a principle in the soul higher than all nature and generation, through which we can be united to the gods, rise above the order of the world and share eternal life; when it is active the soul leaves behind what holds it in generation and gives itself to another order of things.",
  "greek.iamb.8.8":
    "Abammon answers that the gods who revolve in the heavens can both lead fate and free us from it, since there are intelligible principles even in the visible gods; and if only two kinds of gods are admitted, worldly and above the world, the freeing comes from those above the world. He refers Porphyry to his own treatise On the Gods for a fuller account. It is not holy to say with Homer that the gods can be bent: the rites follow pure and intellectual laws, and the gods do not change. From the first, divinity sent souls here so that they might return to it, so the descent and ascent of souls do not oppose each other.",
  // ---- Section IX
  "greek.iamb.9.1":
    "Abammon answers about the personal daemon that the inquiry is of two kinds: one theurgic, drawing the daemon down from higher causes and worshipping it in a universal, supernatural way; the other technical, working from the visible cycles of generation and using horoscopes. Porphyry, he says, has wrongly moved a more perfect sacred work down to a human art and asked his questions about that.",
  "greek.iamb.9.2":
    "Abammon answers that Porphyry has taken only a small part of the subject. Those who work technically invoke the daemon from the decans, the givers of influences, the zodiac, the stars, the sun and moon, the two Bears, the elements and the whole world, yet Porphyry asks only about the lord of the nativity: how he gives the daemon, whether casting nativities is real, and whether the lord of the nativity can be found. Knowing how the daemon comes to be adds nothing to its being, just as things in nature exist whether or not we know how they are produced.",
  "greek.iamb.9.3":
    "Abammon answers the claim that whoever learns his horoscope and knows his own daemon is happy and freed from fate. That contradicts itself: if the daemon is given by the horoscope, knowing it cannot free us from fate. In fact the personal daemon does not come wholly from the horoscope; its origin is older. Whether or not the arts of calculating nativities can be known, the outflow from the stars gives us the daemon all the same, and divine divination teaches about the stars more truly than any calculation.",
  "greek.iamb.9.4":
    "Abammon answers that astrology cannot be rejected just because people disagree about it or because Chaeremon or someone else wrote against it; every science has countless opponents. Astrology is true, though those who miss its aim contradict it; in all the sciences the gods gave, the divine knowledge fades with time as it is mixed with much that is mortal. A small clear sign of its truth remains: it predicts eclipses of the sun and moon and the moon's meetings with the fixed stars, and sight confirms the predictions, while the observations of the Chaldeans and the Egyptians over all of time bear witness to it.",
  "greek.iamb.9.5":
    "Abammon answers the claim that astrologers themselves admit the lord of the nativity can hardly be found, yet say the personal daemon is known from it. Astrologers in fact give clear methods for finding it, some offering five rules, some more, some fewer. Even if it cannot be found by casting the nativity, the lord of the nativity and the daemon he gives still exist, and sacred divination or theurgy may know a great deal about them. In any case the daemon is not given by the lord of the nativity alone but by many more universal principles, and this whole approach treats the daemon in a merely technical, human way.",
  "greek.iamb.9.6":
    "Abammon answers by giving the truth about the personal daemon. It is not assigned to us from one part of the heavens or one visible element, but from the whole world, its many-sided life and the body through which the soul comes down into generation. The daemon is set in the pattern before the soul descends; once the soul takes it as leader, it presides over the soul, completes its lives, binds it to the body, guides its particular life and gives us the starting points of all our thoughts. It governs us until, through priestly theurgy, we gain a god as the overseer and leader of the soul; then the daemon gives way to the higher power, or serves under it.",
  "greek.iamb.9.7":
    "Abammon answers that the personal daemon rules not over one part of us but over all of them at once. Porphyry's report that different daemons preside over health, bodily form and bodily habits, with one over them all, actually shows that there is one daemon guarding everything in us: it is absurd that the living being should be one while its governor is many, since governors are always simpler than what they govern. Nor are some personal daemons good and others bad: evil daemons nowhere hold a ruling place or share power equally with the good.",
  "greek.iamb.9.8":
    "Abammon answers that Porphyry's turn to the philosophers' view, that the daemon is a part of the soul such as the intellect and that whoever has a wise intellect is happy, overturns the whole idea of a personal daemon, because then no higher, daemonic order would stand above the human soul, only a part of the soul ruling over the rest.",
  "greek.iamb.9.9":
    "Abammon answers the report that some worship two personal daemons and others three: this is wrong, because it divides the causes that preside over us instead of referring them to one. Each of us has one personal daemon, neither shared by all men nor merely common. It is called on in a common way because the invocation goes through one god, the lord of daemons, who from the beginning assigned each person his daemon and in the rites reveals it; in theurgy secondary powers are always called through primary ones. When the personal daemon is present, it reveals the worship due to it, its name and the way it should be invoked.",
  "greek.iamb.9.10":
    "Abammon answers that this order suits daemons, part of it belonging to those invoked, part coming from older causes and part completing both. Divine and ineffable invocations must not be likened to human ones; men fall into their greatest errors when they judge the great and perfect powers of the daemons from human weakness and small, divided things. With this he ends his answer about the personal daemon.",
  // ---- Section X
  "greek.iamb.10.1":
    "Abammon answers, on happiness, Porphyry's question whether there is some other, hidden way to it. There can be no ascent to happiness on a path that turns away from the gods: the being and perfection of all good lie in the gods, their first and ancient power is with the priests, and in union with the higher powers are found the contemplation of truth and intellectual knowledge. Knowing the gods goes together with turning towards and knowing ourselves.",
  "greek.iamb.10.2":
    "Abammon answers that one whose intellect is turned to the gods has no leisure to look down to human praise, and that the soul does not invent great things from chance events, since the image-making faculty is never stirred when the intellectual life is fully active, and truth dwells with the gods. The slanders that arrogant men bring against worshippers of the gods have nothing to do with true theology and theurgy; if bad practices spring up in the sacred sciences, as bad arts spring up beside other arts, they are more opposed to those sciences than to anything else.",
  "greek.iamb.10.3":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's comparison of divine prophecy with other ways of foretelling. A natural instinct for the future, like the foreknowledge animals have of earthquakes, winds or storms, comes from keen senses or sympathy and has nothing holy or supernatural in it. Nor is conjecture from signs, as when doctors foresee a fever from the pulse, anything noble: it is human reasoning about natural things. No natural foresight implanted in us in the realm of generation is a true, perfect and eternal good.",
  "greek.iamb.10.4":
    "Abammon answers that only divine divination, joined to the gods, truly gives us a divine life, sharing in the gods' foreknowledge and thought and making us godlike. Those who have it are not, as Porphyry supposes, unhappy foreseers who cannot use what they know: with the foreknowledge they receive beauty, true order and benefit. When ignorance of the future helps the soul grow in virtue the gods hide what is to come; when foreknowledge helps the soul's salvation and return, they place it in the depths of the soul.",
  "greek.iamb.10.5":
    "Abammon answers Porphyry's request to be shown the way to happiness and what it consists in. The more divine, intelligible man, once united to the gods by seeing them, afterwards entered another soul fitted to the human form and so was bound by necessity and fate. The only release from these bonds is knowledge of the gods: to know the good is the form of happiness, as forgetting the good is the form of evil. The one is knowledge of the father and leads the soul back to him; the other is a departure from him and draws man down to what is always flowing. This is the first path to happiness. The priestly and theurgic gift of happiness is called the gate to the maker of all things, or the seat or palace of the good: it purifies the soul, fits the reasoning power to see the good, and finally unites the soul with the gods who give every good.",
  "greek.iamb.10.6":
    "Abammon answers that after joining the soul to the parts of the universe and the divine powers that run through it, theurgy leads the soul to the whole maker of the world, frees it from all matter and unites it with eternal reason alone, connecting it with the self-begotten, self-moved god and his sustaining, intellectual, ordering and demiurgic powers. For the Egyptians this is the end of the priestly raising of the soul to the divine.",
  "greek.iamb.10.7":
    "Abammon answers that the Egyptians hold one good to be divine, the god prior to the intelligible, and the other human, union with that god; Bitys set out these two kinds of good from the Hermetic books. Theurgists do not trouble the divine intellect about trifles but consult it about the purification, freeing and salvation of the soul, and those who have overcome the deceitful daemonic nature and risen to the intelligible and divine are not deceived by a lying daemon.",
  "greek.iamb.10.8":
    "Abammon answers, in closing, that he has replied as well as he can to Porphyry's questions about divination and theurgy, and prays that the gods will keep his true thoughts unchanged, set truth in him for ever, and give him a fuller share in more perfect conceptions of the gods, in which lies the most blessed end of our good and the strengthening of the two men's friendship.",
};
